1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,313
Tonight, on a mountain in Chile,

2
00:00:02,313 --> 00:00:06,374
a telescope notices that one ordinary star
is getting brighter.

3
00:00:06,694 --> 00:00:09,381
Nothing is exploding. Nothing is burning.

4
00:00:09,701 --> 00:00:11,449
The star is just... brighter.

5
00:00:12,449 --> 00:00:15,684
A few weeks later, the star next to it
does the same thing.

6
00:00:16,004 --> 00:00:17,385
Then the one after that.

7
00:00:17,705 --> 00:00:19,749
Something is moving across the sky,

8
00:00:19,749 --> 00:00:22,552
and everything behind it lights up as it
passes.

9
00:00:23,152 --> 00:00:26,935
It isn't a planet. It isn't a comet. It
gives off no light at all.

10
00:00:27,255 --> 00:00:28,317
It's a black hole.

11
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:33,099
Ten times the mass of the Sun, crushed
into a ball sixty kilometres wide.

12
00:00:33,699 --> 00:00:34,944
And it's heading for us.

13
00:00:35,264 --> 00:00:37,746
This is what the next thirty years would
look like.

14
00:00:38,066 --> 00:00:40,406
At the end, there are three ways it can
go.

15
00:00:40,726 --> 00:00:42,661
The most likely one is the strangest.

16
00:00:47,761 --> 00:00:50,113
First, how do you find something that
doesn't shine?

17
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:53,892
You can't see a black hole. You can only
see what it does to light.

18
00:00:54,212 --> 00:00:58,901
Its gravity bends the light of every star
behind it, like a lens made of nothing.

19
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:01,238
When it slides in front of a star,

20
00:01:01,238 --> 00:01:04,798
it focuses that star's light toward us.
And the star flares.

21
00:01:05,398 --> 00:01:06,603
This isn't made up.

22
00:01:06,924 --> 00:01:10,989
In twenty twenty-two, astronomers
confirmed the first black hole

23
00:01:10,989 --> 00:01:14,991
ever found drifting alone through our
galaxy, exactly this way.

24
00:01:15,311 --> 00:01:19,328
A distant star brightened for months. The
thing doing it was invisible.

25
00:01:19,648 --> 00:01:22,177
It's about five thousand light-years away,

26
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:24,766
moving at forty-five kilometres per
second.

27
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:28,347
Ours, in this story, is moving at that
same speed.

28
00:01:28,347 --> 00:01:32,342
But it's only three hundred times farther
from the Sun than we are.

29
00:01:32,662 --> 00:01:34,648
Do the maths, and you get your countdown.

30
00:01:34,968 --> 00:01:36,111
About thirty years.

31
00:01:36,431 --> 00:01:39,157
Astronomers would know the path within
weeks.

32
00:01:39,157 --> 00:01:42,187
You'd find out on the news. Probably on a
Tuesday.

33
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:44,570
One more thing before we go on.

34
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:49,756
Every orbit you'll see in this video comes
from a real gravity simulation,

35
00:01:49,756 --> 00:01:54,424
run for this story. Ten solar masses,
forty-five kilometres per second,

36
00:01:54,424 --> 00:01:57,317
passing the Sun at roughly Earth's
distance.

37
00:01:57,637 --> 00:02:00,891
We didn't choose what happens to the
planets. The maths did.

38
00:02:01,491 --> 00:02:03,408
So what would we do with thirty years?

39
00:02:03,728 --> 00:02:06,750
You can't push a black hole. Every rocket
ever built,

40
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:10,228
fired at once, wouldn't move it in any way
you could measure.

41
00:02:10,548 --> 00:02:12,004
You can't move the Earth either.

42
00:02:12,324 --> 00:02:14,891
And there's nowhere to run. Mars, the
Moon,

43
00:02:14,891 --> 00:02:17,815
every world we could reach sits in the
same path.

44
00:02:18,415 --> 00:02:21,256
So the plan would be the oldest one we
have. Dig.

45
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:26,822
Shelters deep underground, sealed, and run
on nuclear and geothermal power.

46
00:02:26,822 --> 00:02:32,208
Seed vaults. Libraries. Thirty years to
build the deepest refuges in history.

47
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:36,161
And the strangest part is how normal most
of those years would feel.

48
00:02:36,761 --> 00:02:40,696
Because for most of those years, nothing
happens. That's the cruel part.

49
00:02:41,016 --> 00:02:43,836
The sky looks normal. The seasons come and
go.

50
00:02:44,156 --> 00:02:48,031
Then, about two and a half years out, the
black hole reaches the planets.

51
00:02:48,631 --> 00:02:49,967
Neptune goes first.

52
00:02:50,287 --> 00:02:53,775
It has circled the Sun on the same path
for billions of years.

53
00:02:54,095 --> 00:02:58,189
Now something ten times heavier than the
Sun is pulling on it from the dark.

54
00:02:58,509 --> 00:03:00,646
Its orbit stretches. Then it breaks.

55
00:03:00,966 --> 00:03:05,311
Neptune is thrown out of the Solar System,
and nobody on Earth feels a thing.

56
00:03:05,911 --> 00:03:09,967
Beyond Neptune, billions of icy bodies are
scattered like gravel.

57
00:03:10,287 --> 00:03:13,446
Some are flung into deep space. Some are
aimed inward,

58
00:03:13,446 --> 00:03:15,610
though they'd take decades to arrive.

59
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:17,575
They are the least of our problems.

60
00:03:18,175 --> 00:03:21,055
Seven months out, it crosses the orbit of
Saturn.

61
00:03:21,375 --> 00:03:24,721
Here's something you might not expect.
Saturn's rings survive.

62
00:03:25,041 --> 00:03:29,717
They sit so close to the planet that even
a black hole can't peel them away.

63
00:03:29,717 --> 00:03:32,425
Saturn is simply carried off, rings and
all.

64
00:03:33,025 --> 00:03:35,257
Four months later, it reaches Jupiter.

65
00:03:35,577 --> 00:03:40,181
Jupiter is the heaviest thing in the Solar
System after the Sun. It doesn't matter.

66
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:43,032
It keeps its closest moons. But Ganymede,

67
00:03:43,032 --> 00:03:47,600
the largest moon in the Solar System, is
pulled away and never comes back.

68
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,963
From your back garden, this is when it
becomes real.

69
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:54,228
Jupiter, a bright point you've seen your
whole life,

70
00:03:54,228 --> 00:03:57,287
starts wandering across the sky in a way
it never has.

71
00:03:57,607 --> 00:03:59,650
And if you knew exactly where to look,

72
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:02,662
through binoculars, you could see the
black hole itself.

73
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:07,249
Or rather, the hole it makes. A tiny ring
where the stars are smeared out,

74
00:04:07,249 --> 00:04:11,690
about a thirtieth the width of the Moon,
crawling through the constellations.

75
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:13,159
Thirty days out,

76
00:04:13,159 --> 00:04:16,907
the black hole is still more than twice as
far from us as the Sun is.

77
00:04:17,227 --> 00:04:18,645
But it's ten times heavier.

78
00:04:18,645 --> 00:04:21,691
So it's already pulling on Earth harder
than the Sun does.

79
00:04:22,011 --> 00:04:25,260
Earth is being tugged two ways. And the
Sun is losing.

80
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,653
The first thing you'd notice is the sea.

81
00:04:27,973 --> 00:04:32,107
Tides normally come from the Moon. Now
they come from something much heavier.

82
00:04:32,427 --> 00:04:34,305
A metre higher. Then five.

83
00:04:34,625 --> 00:04:37,199
In the last days before the closest pass,

84
00:04:37,199 --> 00:04:42,097
the tides are dozens of metres high, twice
a day, on every coastline on Earth.

85
00:04:42,697 --> 00:04:47,461
Then it passes us. At its closest, it's
less than half as far away as the Sun.

86
00:04:47,781 --> 00:04:50,119
And at that range, it takes something from
us.

87
00:04:50,719 --> 00:04:54,642
At that distance, Earth's gravity can no
longer hold on to the Moon.

88
00:04:54,962 --> 00:04:58,894
After four and a half billion years, the
Moon drifts away from us for good.

89
00:04:59,994 --> 00:05:03,141
Then the black hole swings inward, between
us and the Sun.

90
00:05:03,461 --> 00:05:06,757
In our sky, it sits just a few degrees
from the Sun.

91
00:05:06,757 --> 00:05:09,229
Invisible. Only its pull gives it away.

92
00:05:09,829 --> 00:05:11,470
Then comes the closest pass.

93
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:12,897
It's over in days.

94
00:05:12,897 --> 00:05:17,200
The black hole swings by at well over a
hundred kilometres per second.

95
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:19,241
And it takes Earth's orbit with it.

96
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:22,107
And the black hole doesn't leave
empty-handed.

97
00:05:22,427 --> 00:05:25,750
In our simulation, it captures Venus. A
whole planet,

98
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:29,073
now circling a black hole, carried off
into the dark.

99
00:05:29,393 --> 00:05:33,871
As for us, what happens next depends on a
few hundred thousand kilometres,

100
00:05:33,871 --> 00:05:34,718
here or there.

101
00:05:35,038 --> 00:05:36,403
So there are three endings.

102
00:05:37,603 --> 00:05:39,862
The rarest ending. Earth falls in.

103
00:05:40,182 --> 00:05:43,276
Not straight in. Nothing falls straight
into a black hole.

104
00:05:43,596 --> 00:05:47,454
About a million kilometres out, the pull
on Earth's near side beats

105
00:05:47,454 --> 00:05:51,140
the pull on its far side by so much that
the planet comes apart.

106
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:54,434
Oceans. Crust. Mantle. The iron core.

107
00:05:54,754 --> 00:05:57,409
All of it unwound into a long glowing
stream,

108
00:05:57,409 --> 00:06:00,833
wrapping around the black hole like thread
around a spool.

109
00:06:01,153 --> 00:06:04,245
As it spirals in, it heats up to millions
of degrees.

110
00:06:04,565 --> 00:06:06,456
The last thing Earth ever does is shine.

111
00:06:06,776 --> 00:06:11,213
But a black hole is a very small target.
So this is the least likely ending.

112
00:06:11,813 --> 00:06:15,855
In the second ending, Earth stays with the
Sun. But not on the path it had.

113
00:06:16,175 --> 00:06:18,873
Our orbit today is almost a perfect
circle.

114
00:06:18,873 --> 00:06:22,700
Stretch it into a long oval, and the year
changes completely.

115
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:28,336
Summer comes when Earth swings in close.
The seas steam into a thick, scalding sky.

116
00:06:28,656 --> 00:06:31,739
Then months later, out on the far end of
the oval,

117
00:06:31,739 --> 00:06:34,945
a winter colder than Antarctica.
Everywhere at once.

118
00:06:35,265 --> 00:06:37,867
Some people might survive this.
Underground,

119
00:06:37,867 --> 00:06:39,819
in the right places, for a while.

120
00:06:40,139 --> 00:06:41,596
This is the lucky ending.

121
00:06:42,196 --> 00:06:44,668
But the most likely ending is the third
one.

122
00:06:44,988 --> 00:06:48,403
The black hole throws Earth out of the
Solar System entirely.

123
00:06:48,723 --> 00:06:52,131
We leave the Sun behind at tens of
kilometres per second.

124
00:06:52,451 --> 00:06:56,620
Within a year, it gives us about a
fiftieth of the light it does today.

125
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:59,909
Within decades, it's just the brightest
star in the sky.

126
00:07:00,509 --> 00:07:04,536
The oceans freeze over. The lights of the
cities go out, one by one.

127
00:07:04,856 --> 00:07:06,481
Over the following centuries,

128
00:07:06,481 --> 00:07:09,395
it gets colder than anywhere on Earth has
ever been.

129
00:07:09,995 --> 00:07:12,114
Cold enough that the air itself freezes,

130
00:07:12,434 --> 00:07:14,045
and falls to the ground as snow.

131
00:07:15,445 --> 00:07:16,159
And yet.

132
00:07:16,479 --> 00:07:19,832
Under kilometres of ice, the deep ocean
doesn't freeze.

133
00:07:19,832 --> 00:07:22,453
Heat from Earth's own core keeps it
liquid.

134
00:07:22,773 --> 00:07:25,354
Down there, at volcanic vents on the sea
floor,

135
00:07:25,354 --> 00:07:29,198
there are creatures that have never seen
sunlight. They don't need it.

136
00:07:29,518 --> 00:07:31,355
They would carry on in the dark,

137
00:07:31,355 --> 00:07:34,913
on a planet drifting between the stars,
for billions of years.

138
00:07:35,513 --> 00:07:37,641
Planets like that might not even be rare.

139
00:07:37,961 --> 00:07:40,637
Astronomers now think there may be more
planets

140
00:07:40,637 --> 00:07:43,769
drifting alone through our galaxy than
there are stars.

141
00:07:44,369 --> 00:07:45,408
So, should you worry?

142
00:07:45,728 --> 00:07:49,532
Our galaxy probably holds around a hundred
million black holes.

143
00:07:49,532 --> 00:07:50,981
We've found a few dozen.

144
00:07:51,301 --> 00:07:54,314
The closest one we know of is about one
thousand five

145
00:07:54,314 --> 00:07:57,782
hundred and sixty light-years away. And it
isn't coming here.

146
00:07:58,382 --> 00:08:00,961
For a black hole to pass this close to the
Sun,

147
00:08:00,961 --> 00:08:04,472
you'd expect to wait something like a
hundred quadrillion years.

148
00:08:04,792 --> 00:08:08,032
Millions of times longer than the universe
has existed.

149
00:08:08,632 --> 00:08:11,687
So tonight, if a star looks a little
brighter than it should...

150
00:08:12,387 --> 00:08:13,914
it's almost certainly nothing.

151
00:08:14,614 --> 00:08:15,337
Almost.

152
00:08:17,337 --> 00:08:19,892
Next time, we stop watching from a
distance.

153
00:08:20,392 --> 00:08:24,804
We drop a ship into the biggest planet we
have. A world with no ground at all.

154
00:08:26,404 --> 00:08:30,138
There's a lot more out there in the dark.
Subscribe, and we'll keep looking.

